{
 "id": "greensleeves",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": false,
 "title": "Greensleeves",
 "description": "The English tune of the 1500s in its own old words, \"Alas, my love, you do me wrong\", for 2 to 4 singers: the first verse and the refrain, \"Greensleeves was all my joy\". The soprano sings the tune; the alto, tenor and bass sing the words beneath it on the harmony G. A. Macfarren wrote for it in the 1850s, the inner parts moving mostly by step. Gentle and lilting, in a slow 6/8.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "folk"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 60,
 "key": "F#m",
 "timeSignature": "6/8",
 "pickupBeats": 0.333333,
 "credits": {
  "words": "traditional (English, 16th century), as printed in William Chappell, Popular Music of the Olden Time (1855 to 1859)",
  "tune": "traditional (English, 16th century)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella, on G. A. Macfarren's harmony (1855 to 1859)"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: traditional, 16th century (the ballad licensed in 1580; printed by Chappell in the 1850s)",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional, 16th century",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella on G. A. Macfarren's piano harmony in Chappell's Popular Music of the Olden Time (public domain: published 1855 to 1859, Macfarren died 1887, Chappell 1888): the bass sings the piano's lowest note (an octave higher on \"company\", and the chord's third under \"sleeves was my\" in the last line), the alto and tenor are new, one note a syllable; the tune a whole tone higher, \"company\" in three syllables as Bridge prints it (1923), and breaths written in for beginners",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "William Chappell, Popular Music of the Olden Time, vol. 1 (London: Cramer, Beale & Chappell, [1859?]; issued in parts from 1855), \"Tune of Green Sleeves. Oldest copy.\", p. 230, the harmony by G. A. Macfarren, voice and piano in E minor: the tune, verse 1 and the refrain, and Macfarren's harmony, a whole tone higher in F sharp minor. University of Toronto (Robarts Library) copy, archive.org/details/popularmusicofol01chapuoft (page image 0262). The tune as sung today checked against Frederick Bridge, Shakespearean Music in the Plays and Early Operas (London and Toronto: J. M. Dent & Sons, 1923), \"Greensleeves (The generally accepted Version)\", p. 79, University of Victoria Libraries copy, archive.org/details/shakespeareanmus0000brid (page image 0099), whose \"com-pa-ny\" in three syllables is sung here; not a modern edition"
 },
